 [:db/cas [:question/id #uuid "1aea0f50-2894-540a-8561-3e930ff1c7d7"] :question/problem "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?" "Given that the tangent line approximation to a differentiable function $f(x)$ at every point in an open interval containing $x=\\sqrt{2}$ strictly overestimates the values of the function at every other point in that interval, which of the following must be true?"]
 [:db/cas [:question/id #uuid "1aea0f50-2894-540a-8561-3e930ff1c7d7"] :question/worked-solution "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at every point $a$ in the given neighborhood strictly overestimates the values of the function at every other point, we have\n\n$$\nL_a(x)>f(x)\\quad (x\\ne a),\n$$\n\nthat is, each tangent line $L_a(x)$ lies above the graph of $f(x)$ throughout the neighborhood, with equality only at $x=a.$ A differentiable function whose graph lies below each of its tangent lines on an interval is concave down on that interval.\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$"]
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "1aea0f50-2894-540a-8561-3e930ff1c7d7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
