 [:db/cas [:question/id #uuid "d8cd36a0-19ac-53de-ada3-7c525383dea4"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,x,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1-\\frac{1}{2}x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,x,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1-\\frac{1}{2}x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?"]
 [:db/cas [:question/id #uuid "d8cd36a0-19ac-53de-ada3-7c525383dea4"] :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=x,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=x,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-x\\cdot 0+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=x,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=x,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-x\\cdot 0+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
