 [:db/cas [:question/id #uuid "ba66d242-26a0-594d-82a7-1f167d592d18"] :question/problem "The rate of growth of a car's displacement increases at a rate that's proportional to the time $t$ since the car started moving. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the car's displacement, $D,$ at time $t?$" "A car's displacement increases at a rate that's proportional to the time $t$ since the car started moving. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the car's displacement, $D,$ at time $t?$"]
 [:db/cas [:question/id #uuid "ba66d242-26a0-594d-82a7-1f167d592d18"] :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The rate of growth of a **car's displacement** $D(t)$ **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **time** $t$ since the car started moving.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *car's displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *interval of time* since the car started moving. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the car's displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt.\n$$" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*A **car's displacement** $D(t)$ **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **time** $t$ since the car started moving.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *car's displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *interval of time* since the car started moving. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the car's displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt.\n$$"]
